/* book Worked example 2: Q: A NAND3 pull-down has three nMOS of 2 kΩ each when on. The output node has 30 fF; the two internal nodes have 5 fF each. All internal nodes start charged. Find the Elmore delay of the falling output. How much of it is due to the internal nodes?
   1. Name the nodes from the bottom: x1 (above the lowest transistor), x2, then the output.
   2. x1 discharges through 2 kΩ, x2 through 4 kΩ, the output through 6 kΩ.
   3. τD = 5 fF × 2 kΩ + 5 fF × 4 kΩ + 30 fF × 6 kΩ = 10 + 20 + 180 = 210 ps.
   4. 50% delay ≈ 0.69 × 210 = 145 ps.
   5. Internal nodes contribute 30 ps of the 210 ps, 14%. If the lower inputs were already high so x1 and x2 were at 0 V before the top input rises, only 180 ps would remain: the reason to put the latest input at the top.
   Answer: τD = 210 ps, a 50% delay of about 145 ps; the internal nodes add 30 ps (14%). */
