/* book: lead: Every CMOS chip draws current from its supply for two reasons. When nodes switch, the supply charges their capacitance and that energy ends up as heat: this is dynamic power, and it grows with the clock frequency. Even when nothing switches, transistors that are meant to be off still leak: this is static power. Dynamic power sets how hot a busy processor runs; static power sets how fast an idle ph */
/* book Figure 3.7.1: Power of the example block against clock frequency on log scales. Switching power rises in proportion to f and passes 1.62 mW at 100 MHz; leakage is flat, so below the crossover (1.11 MHz, or 11.1 MHz with the lower-threshold transistors) leakage is the larger part. */
